A||||6||PaUL||Weight Complaint
Z||000000||PaUL||04-09-99||05:40 PM||||For a plastic car, the Fiero sure weighs alot, almost 3000lbs, isnt that right? I guess GM had to do this for crashworthiness. If the Fiero weighed about 800lbs less, it would have been perfect. I have Brooklands Fiero book. In it is described an experimental Fiero with an all aluminum frame made by ALCAN. The book says that the frame was made with production stampings but instead of welding, the stamping were glued together. If GM had produced this car with a little more powerful engine, they would have had an Acura NSX killer for less than half the price.....too bad. 
Z||000001||theogre||04-10-99||08:12 PM||||There has been allot of research into aluminum cars, including the concept Fiero you mention. Fact is, Aluminum framing systems are Not cheaper to make than steel. Chrysler Prowler is in limited production partly because working with aluminum in production is extreamly dificult.<p>Ridgidity problems with aluminum are also a problem. Often by the time you get AL to form like steel, you have an alloy that will deform over time from constant pounding like what a car goes thruough.<p>Bonding components, prety much limited to epoxies in the 80's, where not up to long term use, or mass production, of cars. For example of long term component problems look ad the Fiero Rear Deck lid. This single component of the car has a number of bonded joints. The bonds do hold up over time, but the bonding method altered the materials, resulting in externally visible lines on the lid.
Z||000002||Motorhead||04-11-99||05:54 AM||||I got into lengthy dscussion with an engineer at work recently about the use of aluminum over steel in the manufacture of automotive chassis'. His biggest concern was that of longevity. It seems aluminum is highly susceptible to long term stress failure. This is evident in the aircraft industry. Airplanes are subjected to frequent x-ray and dye inspections for detection of stress cracks in their aluminum airframes from constant loads placed on their components.<p>His opinion on aluminum in an automotive application was that it would make for a really light weight chassis requiring very high maintenance. Racing use only!<p>
Z||000003||FieroMike||04-12-99||10:53 AM||||Paul,<br>For the size of the car and HP of the engine, 3,000lbs is light.  My car being as light as it is has problems at high speeds staying glued to the road, I sometimes wish it was a little heavier.  Just my .02 :-)
Z||000004||PaUL||04-12-99||02:38 PM||||An aluminum frame for a prod. car can work, Honda, Audi and others have proved it, but the cars made are very expensive high end models. I think GM was trying a different cheaper approach by gluing the frame together. As for longeivity, I think the steel frame car will rust away long before the aluminum one is cracked to bits. 
Z||000005||PaUL||04-12-99||02:40 PM||||FieroMike, sound like you could use better aerodynamics. What if you angled the rear wing more?
Z||000006||theogre||04-12-99||10:26 PM||||AL alloys have 2 problems in cars. <p>they are very brittle, which makes them hard to work with in production, and causes cracking over time which makes things break.<p>they are very soft, which makes them formable with the same dies as steel, but also lets them continue bending over time, ruining fram alignment.<p>Atatching AL parts can be annoying, but isn't that dificult. It's the properties of AL that make it expensive to use. anywhere from 1.5-5x the cost of steel depending on the application.<p>You'll note that most AL framing on "production" cars is done on low volume cars, like Prowler that only produced around 400 units in 97. (there was no 98) Or it is used on High dollar cars that are statisticaly likely to only be kept a couple years by their owners. (The majority of luxury cars are kept only 2-4 years.)<p>Another place you will see allot of AL is in aportioned, "Semi", truck trailers. The size of these beams, and the way trailers are loaded, make them an excelent candidate for AL. You will likely Never see it used for the "tractor" frame which takes considerably diferant kinds of loads, and often see very high loads on very small areas.
